Keelhaus
Ingredients: CHICAGO HOUSE & GARAGE HOUSE + CINEMATIC, EPIC & ORCHESTRAL + SPITFIRE LABS AMBIENT GUITARS + 2010s ERA  
Creative Catalyst: The genre was born at an illegal rooftop rave in a flooded megacity, 2091.

Keelhaus sounds like a classical conservatory and a South Side warehouse collapsing into four fathoms of warm Atlantic water. It matches the skippy, syncopated swing of late-90s garage and the relentless four-on-the-floor heart of Chicago house against monolithic, tear-stained symphonic brass and sweeping cellos. Tying this clash together are the crystalline, drifting textures of Spitfire LABS ambient guitars and 2010s-style hyper-sidechained vocal chops, creating a sound that feels both hydro-ballistic and intimately heartbroken.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 127–131 BPM. The rhythm marries the heavy, round kick drum of Chicago house with the nervous, displaced shuffles and swung hi-hats of UK garage, accented by deep sub-bass drops timed to mimic the rhythmic thud of industrial bilge pumps.
- Harmonic Palette: Drenched in Dorian and Aeolian minor keys with sudden, ecstatic lifts into Lydian major chords. Bittersweet minor 9ths and suspended 4ths migrate between a grand string section and tape-saturated electric guitar drones.
- Production Signatures: 
  1. Tidal Sidechaining: The entire mix—especially the orchestral string sections and ambient guitar washes—is violently ducked not just to the kick drum, but to an undulating, low-frequency pressure oscillator that mimics the swell of incoming tides.
  2. Brine-Siphon Phasing (Impossible Technique): Producers route their master drum buss through an open-air copper coil submerged in saltwater cisterns on building rooftops, physically altering electrical impedance and phasing the audio as ambient humidity and wave height fluctuate.
  3. Granular Drift Choirs: Pitched vocal snippets reminiscent of 2010s post-dubstep are smeared through endless reverb algorithms until they behave like marine choir pads beneath Spitfire guitar harmonics.
- Dynamics: Songs open like calm water at low tide—sparse ambient guitar swells, distant horn drones, and echoing dripping sounds—before bursting into urgent, syncopated garage grooves that stack orchestral brass into towering, breathless crescendos.

Scene & Origin Lore
In late 2091, after the third sea-wall failure submerged Manhattan up to the 40th floor, water-taxi smugglers, displaced conservatory players, and runway dropouts began mooring their skiffs to the antennae arrays of abandoned skyscrapers. Keelhaus was birthed during a legendary forty-eight-hour storm on the heli-pad of the old municipal tower. The youth wear tailored dive-suits beneath torn Victorian evening wear, salt-crusted trench coats cinched with climbing carabiners, and industrial ear protection lined with velvet. Dancing is tight, bouncy, and defensive—known as "skiff-stepping"—designed to maintain footing on wet, algae-filmed concrete while the Atlantic boils three hundred feet below.

Typical Instruments: Roland TR-909 (heavily saturated and filtered), Spitfire LABS Ambient Guitars (bowed and granularized), live 16-piece orchestral string section, pitched vocal samplers, hydrophone recordings, fretless bass, and treated French horns.

For Fans Of: Burial, Floating Points, Max Richter, Todd Edwards.

