- Story & Context: Decades after the lower borough vanished completely beneath the Atlantic, salvage divers maintained that the copper spire of the old communications tower still pulsed with an eerie, rhythmic low-end. The myth claims that during the final total blackout of 2091, Cistern Syndicate welded their audio outputs directly into the marine navigation beacon, transmitting a gargantuan orchestral house hymn until the rising water swallowed their amplifiers. Scavengers later found only rusted turntable needles, broken cello bows, and a grease-pencil scrawl left on the radio console that became scene gospel. Even now, cargo captains navigate the dark reefs by following the distorted rhythm and the looped vocal crying: "We built our church where the sirens go to die."
